  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 AM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nToday through Sat night...A trough will enter the Canadian west\ncoast tonight, shifting airflow to more southwest and \nincreasing its strength. This will still allow some isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms for the afternoon and evening tonight.\nWind will also be much stronger than the barely there wind of \nthe previous 6 days--not that it is approaching Wind Advisory. \nThe airflow is also bringing in slightly warmer air, and this \nwill be the warmest day of the next seven. SPC analysis is that \nthere is a marginal risk of severe thunderstorms in the \nsoutheast highlands for today and tonight.\n\nThe model run tonight is doing things a little different. In the\npast few days, this trough was staying in southern Canada.\nHowever, tonight the trough turns into a closed low and digs\nsouthward into the American Pacific Northwest, and is still\nthere on Sat afternoon. Strong upper level wind continues, and\nquite a bit more moisture is flowing into southern Idaho.\nPrecipitable water over all but the central Idaho mountains\nexceeds or hovers around 1 inch, which indicates heavy rains. On\nthe more positive side, extensive cloud cover could reduce the\ncoverage of thunderstorms, and right now it appears as though\nscattered thunderstorms will be limited both Fri and Sat to the\neast and southeast highlands, mostly. \n\nExtensive cloud cover, the troughing getting closer than\nprevious model runs, and evaporative cooling is now generating a\ntremendous cool-down from today to Fri. 8 to 12 deg F from one\nday to the next, then another 1 to 5 deg F cooling from Fri to \nSat. Overnight lows will cool substantially from tonight to Fri\nnight, and then small drop Fri to Sat night, just not of the \nsame magnitude as the high temperatures.\n\nThe closer proximity of the low now means overall stronger\nsurface wind is likely. Sat and Sat night look particularly\nmoderate at least for the Snake River plain, while today and Fri\nwill only be that way in the afternoon.\n\nSun through Wed...On Sun night the closed low will eventually \nmove to the east of Idaho. In its wake is a dry westerly flow.\nTemperatures continue in the cooling trend until Sun afternoon.\nThen clearer skies and strong sunshine will bring back a warming\ntrend; by Mon, temperatures are back to near normal for the time\nof year. Wind will still be moderate to strong each afternoon \nand evening during this period. However, the thunderstorm and \nprecipitation risk drops, well, precipitously, from Mon onward.\nThe upper level ridge axis stays farther east that it has been\nlately, and this puts the monsoonal remnants moving into eastern\nUtah and Wyoming rather than southern Idaho--for most of the\ntime.\n\nA key thing, though, is that this change in the forecast is new.\nWill guidance keep this new closed low scenario, or dump it\nduring the days model runs today. Stay tuned.",
